[size=3]You installed COG. Now what? This is the short path from "I have a mod installed" to "I moved my health bar and added a clock."[/size]

[center][img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/qL0ZKvB1/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Left.png[/img] [size=6]Open the Editor[/size] [img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/gL2J92Hx/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Right.png[/img][/center]

[color=#bf9000][size=5]The Key[/size][/color]

Default editor key is [color=#b45f06]F3[/color]. Press it in-game to open the COG overlay; press it again to close. You can rebind by clicking the key name in the top-right of the panel. Prefer editing JSON by hand? Set [font=Courier New]editorKey[/font] in [font=Courier New]SKSE/Plugins/COG/general.json[/font] (see [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/articles/12505]Intermediate: Configuration On Disk[/url][/b]).

[color=#bf9000][size=5]Help (?)[/size][/color]

The [b]?[/b] button opens About, Definitions, Help, and [b]Suppressions[/b] (live hide map + Alias Index). Worth a skim once so the vocabulary sticks: Gear, Group, Gauge, Gizmo.

[center][img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/qL0ZKvB1/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Left.png[/img] [size=6]The Tabs[/size] [img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/gL2J92Hx/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Right.png[/img][/center]

[color=#bf9000][size=5]What Each One Is For[/size][/color]

[list]
[*][b]General:[/b] Backup theme colours, [color=#b45f06]Show Default Group[/color], default gadget font, and [color=#b45f06]Toggle UI[/color] (optional hotkey + whether Toggle also soft-hides Flash HUD)[/*]
[*][b]Gears:[/b] Edit how a registered tracker looks (icon, colours, hide rules, HUD link). Editing here does [b]not[/b] put it on the HUD by itself[/*]
[*][b]Groups:[/b] Lines of gears — place, order, stack, theme. Open a group accordion to drag it above the editor[/*]
[*][b]Gauges:[/b] One gear, dressed up (meter bar/arc or framed picture/gear)[/*]
[*][b]Gizmos:[/b] Built-ins (Clock, Feed, Shout Meter, Suppressor) and mod-added products[/*]
[/list]

[center][img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/qL0ZKvB1/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Left.png[/img] [size=6]Your First Layout[/size] [img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/gL2J92Hx/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Right.png[/img][/center]

[color=#bf9000][size=5]Groups[/size][/color]

COG creates a locked [b]Default Group[/b] on first load. New third-party gears are offered there once, the first time they're detected. Removing them from the Default Group means they won't reappear there unless you add them back. Builtin COG gears are not auto-placed in any Group, but you can add those yourself.

[list=1]
[*]Open [b]Groups[/b].[/*]
[*]Expand [b]Default Group[/b] (or create another group). If you create a new group, you can optionally click [color=#b45f06]Edit[/color] to rename it.[/*]
[*]Press [color=#b45f06]Add gea[b]r[/b][/color] and pick something (Health, Carry Weight, a mod gear…).[/*]
[*]Drag the Group on the HUD until it's where you'd like, or enter X and Y coordinates manually.[/*]
[*]Tweak icon size, spacing, stack, and colours if you like.[/*]
[/list]

Turn [color=#b45f06]Show Default Group[/color] off in General if you want that line invisible without deleting it.

[color=#bf9000][size=5]Try a Gauge[/size][/color]

[list=1]
[*]Head over to [color=#b45f06]Gauges [/color]→ [color=#b45f06]Add Gauge[/color].[/*]
[*]Bind a single gear (for example Spell Charge).[/*]
[*]Pick Meter (bar/arc) or Frame (picture / gear in a bezel).[/*]
[*]Click on the Gauge to drag, rotate, and resize.[/*]
[/list]

[color=#bf9000][size=5]Try a Gizmo[/size][/color]

[list=1]
[*][color=#b45f06]Gizmos [/color]→ pick from the gallery (left).[/*]
[*]Place [color=#b45f06]Clock [/color]or [color=#b45f06]Feed [/color](or both!) to feel the difference from gears.[/*]
[*]For Feed: enable the sections you want (Notifications, Inventory, …). Each section will suppress or divert the vanilla UI it replaces; see [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/articles/12507]Beginner: HUD Suppression[/url][/b] for more details.[/*]
[/list]

Layout is saved under [font=Courier New]SKSE/Plugins/COG/[/font] on disk, not in your save. Change load orders freely; your HUD arrangement stays put in the mod or Overwrite files.

[center][img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/qL0ZKvB1/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Left.png[/img] [size=6]Optional Next Steps[/size] [img width=250]https://i.ibb.co/gL2J92Hx/Dragonborn-Knotwork-Right.png[/img][/center]

On [color=#b45f06]General[/color], you can bind an optional [color=#b45f06]Toggle UI[/color] hotkey (click clears a bound key; click again to set a new one). It is for a [b]clear view of the world[/b] (photos, immersion) — not a "vanilla HUD mode." It hides all COG play gadgets until you toggle again (same idea as visibility [color=#b45f06]None[/color]). It does [b]not[/b] undo gear HUD links, Feed hides, or the Suppressor: anything already soft-hidden stays soft-hidden. The key only works while [b]playing with menus closed[/b] (not in the console, the COG editor, search fields, or other open menus). By default it also soft-hides Flash HUD menus via alpha (vanilla HUD, TrueHUD, MiniMap, STB widgets, BTPS, and similar) so the screen is emptier; turn [color=#b45f06]Toggle also hides Flash HUD[/color] off if you only want COG gadgets to vanish. Session-only; unrelated to Active Suppressions.

[list]
[*]Love your old [b]iWant Status Bars[/b] addons? Install the optional [b]iWant Integration[/b] from the FOMOD along with any mods that use [b]iWant Widgets/Status Bars[/b]. [b][i]Do not install[/i][/b] [b]iWant Widgets[/b] or [b]iWant Status Bars[/b]. The integration replaces them. Full load-order notes: [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/articles/12508]Beginner: iWant Integration[/url][/b].[/*]
[*]Imported Gears (widgets from iWant) will appear on your Default Group the first time they're loaded. From there, you can rearrange them, create another group to move them to, or alter the group settings to better accommodate your vision.[/*]
[*]iWant ships icon-only, but COG's groups come with a percentage bar by default. If you want numbers to appear differently, or not at all, edit the [color=#b45f06]Number Format[/color] of the group to suit you.[/*]
[/list]

[color=#bf9000][size=5]Adding Your Own Icons, Fonts, and other Settings[/size][/color]

[list]
[*]Want to add custom icons/fonts or ship them in a small mod? See [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/articles/12506]Intermediate: Fonts and Icons[/url][/b].[/*]
[*]Want to hide something you don't like without replacing it? Use the [color=#b45f06]Suppressor Gizmo[/color]. If you need help finding the name of something you want to hide, check the [color=#b45f06]Alias Index[/color] at [color=#b45f06][b]?[/b] → Suppressions[/color] in game to get the correct name.[/*]
[/list]

[color=#bf9000][size=5]When Something Looks Wrong[/size][/color]

[list]
[*]Curious why a vanilla bar vanished? Check out the [color=#b45f06]Active Suppressions[/color] list under [color=#b45f06][b]?[/b] → Suppressions[/color] in game, or [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/articles/12507]Beginner: HUD Suppression[/url][/b].[/*]
[*]Confirm Prisma UI + COG loaded properly by pressing the menu key (F3 by default) and checking the logs if it doesn't show up (check [font=Courier New]COG.log[/font] in your SKSE log directory). SKSE, Address Library, and Prisma UI all need to be active for COG to work.[/*]
[*]Missing a Gear you wanted? Make sure you [i]placed[/i] the gear on a Group or Gauge. Only imported Gears appear in a group right away, and they'll only be visible immediately if the Default Group is turned on.[/*]
[*]After reinstalling COG in MO2, remember Overwrite may still hold your old group, gauge, or gizmo files (those are created at runtime). Shipped files like [font=Courier New]seen.json[/font] live in the mod folder and get wiped with a fresh install. See [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/articles/12505]Intermediate: Configuration On Disk[/url][/b] for more details.[/*]
[*]If something you left in place doesn't look right after an update, you can try:
[list=1]
[*]Resetting the Gear to its defaults on the Gears tab.[/*]
[*]Deleting and re-adding the Gear in its Group.[/*]
[*]Deleting and recreating the relevant Gauge or Gizmo.[/*]
[/list]
[/*]
[/list]
